\medskip
\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\footnotesize
All rows report size (no $p$-hacking). Column 3 is the rejection rate of the projection test,
columns 4--9 are the rejection rates of the six tests from Elliott et al. (2022), and columns
10--12 are the rejection rates of the three tests of Kudrin (2024). All tests are run at the 5\%
level. The sample size is $n = 5000$. For the tests in Elliott et al. (2022) we use their values of
the tuning parameters and their code file Tests.R. For the tests of Kudrin (2024) we use the sinc
kernel with $\kappa = 3$, and the critical value of $T_{\infty}$ comes from 100 nonparametric
bootstrap draws. For the projection test we set $w_j = \min\{1, 0.5^{j - 20}\}$ and $J = 40$. We
form $\mathcal{X}$ using a grid of 3000 elements evenly spaced between $\pm 6.5$ plus the points 0
and 9999. We symmetrize about zero and shift by 1.96 as described in Remarks 12 and 13. We run 100
bootstrap repetitions and 500 simulation repetitions. Projection used \texttt{nnls} package in R by
Mullen and van Stokkum (2024). Transparency R package available at:
\url{https://github.com/sfaridan/When_is_p_hacking_detectable}
\end{minipage}
\end{table}
